  "body": "**C82 < 0.2520591386\\***, 3.46·10⁻⁴ below #330 (R. Pinho, 0.252404806\\*). Our checker gives the rigorous upper bound B ≤ 0.25205913852601. Arhan Canli's independent replay (comment below) encloses B ∈ [0.2520591385259547, 0.2520591385259548].\n\nThis pull request now records three of our certificates as separate rows, all in Doche's limit-point construction and R. Pinho's certificate format. All earlier rows and credits are kept.\n\n| row | bound | what is new |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Pat26] | 0.2534350402\\* | 17 new numerators from the iterated dual-weighted LLL search |\n| [Pat26b] | 0.2524177045\\* | 4 new degree-104 denominators from the CVP-enumeration search |\n| **[Pat26c]** | **0.2520591386\\*** | **3 new degree-104 denominators with normalised log M(R∘T)/208 ≈ 0.251994, 0.252086, 0.252106, below every earlier denominator** |\n\n**The new certificate:**\n- 3 monic degree-104 denominators, D = 999,999,999,960.\n- 21 numerators, deg A/D = 0.9053:\n  - Doche's P1, P2, P3, P4, P6;\n  - Pinho's MS360 (#301);\n  - two earlier library factors;\n  - 13 numerators from our LLL search.\n- Mahler part 0.25203940065826; integral part ≤ 1.974·10⁻⁵.\n\n**How it was found.** We continued our CVP denominator search of [Pat26b]. The starting library was our previous search state plus the six denominators of #330, credited there; none of them is in the final certificate. After two rounds adding single factors, the search reached factors with log M/deg ≈ 0.2521, and each later round lowered the LP by 5·10⁻⁵ to 1·10⁻⁴. The certificate is a fine-grid re-solve of the round-6 state, with exact integer multiplicities and dyadic exponents rounded down. The search was still improving.\n\n**Verification.** `checkers/vcheck.py`, the same checker as [Pat26b] and unchanged, gives B ≤ 0.25205913852601. Its printed ball radius covers Arb's arithmetic only; the integral part is an upper bound at quadrature tolerance 10⁻¹¹, so the value is an upper bound, not a two-sided enclosure. It checks:\n- H1: integer coefficients, monic denominators, positive integer multiplicities, q ≥ 0;\n- H2: deg A < D;\n- H3: exact FLINT gcds for every active pair;\n- Arb root enclosures with multiplicities summing to the degree;\n- an integral upper bound on an exact dyadic tiling (405k panels).\n\nThe float value on 2²⁰ points is 0.2520591385373. Certificate sha256 `00125f6984ad2646a79d8782ee38f5161bd24c17d4899e4af74ff3e529ba1dbe`.\n\nCertificate, checker and receipt: [vidigoat/optimization-records@197962c, tao/certificates/c82/v3](https://github.com/vidigoat/optimization-records/tree/197962c9610f583e9bc56efe37bfb994c1eeb834/tao/certificates/c82/v3).\n\n```\npython checkers/vcheck.py certificate.json --tol 1e-11 --prec 192 --target 0.2520591386\n```\n\n`scripts/check_pages.py`: 0 errors, 0 warnings. The branch is rebased on current main. If #301 or #330 merge first, their rows stay, and the README cell should show the smallest value.\n\nCredit:\n- Doche: the construction and the P-factors.\n- R. Pinho: the certificate format, the joint LP, the residue-class denominator search, the #229/#301 libraries, and the #330 factors in the starting library.\n- Grinsztajn and Épi/BakeLab: earlier factors.\n- Arhan Canli: independent replays of [Pat26], [Pat26b] and #301.\n\nFound and prepared with Claude (Anthropic) agents under V. Patankar's direction; the values are recomputed by the linked checker. They have not been verified line by line by a human expert.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
